window.INTERVIEW_EXECUTION["swift-72"] = {"timeBudget": {"navigation": 4, "solution": 9, "coding": 26, "verification": 6}, "navigation": {"say": "Two questions matter more than the rest. Whether the deadline is inclusive, because it moves the tightest cases by one; and whether the last rule shares any state with the first three, because if it does not then it is a second problem in the same file and should be treated as one.", "questions": ["Is a filing lodged when it finishes exactly on its deadline, or must it finish before?", "Is there one reviewer, and does work ever pause?", "For the settling gap, does the review effort matter at all, or is lodging a filing one slot whatever it took to prepare?", "Do the deadlines apply under the settling-gap rule as well, or is that a separate scheduling question?"], "assumptions": ["The deadline is inclusive: a filing finishing exactly on it is lodged.", "One reviewer, starting at hour zero, never idle, so a missed filing still consumes its hours.", "The settling gap is about lodgement slots rather than review effort, so each filing occupies one hour there whatever its review count.", "Deadlines do not apply under the settling-gap rule, which is what makes it a different objective rather than a harder version of the same one."], "edgeCases": ["an empty backlog, where every answer is empty or zero rather than a failure", "a filing whose review hours already exceed its deadline, which can never be lodged in any order", "a backlog where the first filing is the longest, which is where giving back beats skipping", "a backlog of many distinct types under the settling gap, which is where the skeleton formula alone is too small"], "restate": "Given filings with review hours and statutory deadlines worked by one reviewer, report what a proposed order achieves, the largest number that can be lodged on time, which ones and in what order, and separately the fewest elapsed hours to lodge the whole backlog when filings of one type need a settling gap between them."}, "solution": {"pitch": "Deadline order, take everything, and give back the longest filing taken so far whenever the clock overruns. Shortest-first is the reflex and it is wrong, because a short filing with a distant deadline can always be deferred while a long one with a near deadline cannot. The exchange argument for the give-back is the subtlest claim in the file and it is worth stating slowly: one filing came in as one went out, so the count is unchanged, and the clock is now no higher than it stood before the new filing arrived, so no later decision is ever made worse. The last part is a different animal entirely: nothing is revoked, there is no exchange, and the correctness comes from a counting bound the busiest statutory type imposes.", "dataStructures": ["One array sorted by deadline, which every part of the first three reads.", "A max-heap keyed on review hours, holding the filings currently accepted, so the one worth giving back is always at the top.", "A running integer clock, which is the entire rest of the sweep's state.", "For the last part, a dictionary of counts per statutory type. There is no schedule built at all, which is the point of a counting bound."], "types": ["A filing carries a readable id and a statutory type as strings, because both answers are lists a compliance lead then acts on.", "The trace is its own type with the lodged list, the missed list and the finishing hour, deliberately not a boolean: the sweep needs the hour, the selection check needs the lodged list, and a user needs the missed list.", "The accepted filings go into the heap as a small private Comparable struct rather than bare integers, so the survivors can be named afterwards.", "The failures are a typed enum, including an explicit range guard, because Swift traps on integer overflow rather than returning a wrong number."], "composition": "The first three parts share one sequencing routine, with the count reading its size and the selection reading its ids, so there is no way for them to disagree. The strongest composition here is a call rather than an assertion about shape: the suite replays the reported selection through the first part and requires that nothing is missed, which catches a plausible selection that does not actually fit. The fourth part optimises a different quantity, so no invariant against the second exists at all and inventing one would be a design error; the suite checks it against an independently written hour-by-hour simulation instead.", "tradeoff": "The heap costs a logarithmic factor and an allocation per filing where a plain scan for the longest would be simpler to read. I want it because the give-back is the operation this problem exists to teach and the heap is what makes it a first-class move rather than an afterthought. If the backlogs were always tiny, a linear scan would be honest and I would say so out loud rather than reaching for a heap by habit.", "trap": "Three. Sorting by review hours, which feels like scheduling and loses exactly the filings whose deadlines were tight. Skipping the filing in hand rather than giving back the longest, which is a different and worse algorithm that produces the same answer on easy backlogs. And in the last part, returning the skeleton formula without the clamp, which is wrong on any backlog with enough distinct types to leave no idle hour at all."}, "coding": {"order": ["Write the replay first. It is short, everything else is checked against it, and it forces the deadline convention to be settled.", "Write the deadline ordering next, with the tie-break, and put the validation there.", "Say the exchange argument aloud, then write the sweep with the heap.", "Check it on a backlog where the first filing is the longest, which is where giving back beats skipping.", "Read the selection off the same walk rather than writing a second one, then replay it to prove it fits.", "Write the last part separately and say clearly that it is a different objective with a different kind of proof."], "commentary": ["Deadline order, not shortest first. A short filing with a distant deadline can always wait; a long one with a near deadline cannot, so the deadline is what the key has to be.", "I take every filing as I reach it, which sounds reckless until you see what happens on an overrun.", "When the clock passes the deadline in hand, I give back the longest filing taken so far, and that may well be one I accepted several steps ago.", "Here is why that is safe. One filing came in and one went out, so the count is exactly what it was.", "And the clock is now no higher than it stood before this filing arrived, because what I removed is at least as long as what I added.", "So no future decision is ever made worse, which is what lets a greedy revoke an earlier choice and stay correct.", "Note that the filing in hand is itself a candidate for being given back, and on some backlogs it is the one that goes.", "The last part is a different kind of greedy and I want to name that. Nothing is revoked, there is no exchange, and the whole answer is a counting bound the busiest type imposes.", "The bound alone is not the answer though: with enough distinct types there is no idle hour at all, and the schedule is simply as long as the backlog. The answer is the larger of the two."], "checkIn": "Before the last part I would say: I am reading the settling gap as a rule about lodgement slots, so each filing takes one hour there regardless of its review effort, and deadlines do not apply. That makes it a genuinely separate question from the first three rather than a harder version of them. If the registry rule actually interacts with review time, that is a different and much harder problem and I would rather know now."}, "verification": {"walkthroughs": ["Take four filings: 100 hours due at 200, 200 due at 1300, 1000 due at 1250 and 2000 due at 3200. In deadline order that is the first, the third, the second, the fourth.", "The clock runs 100, then 1100, then 1300, all inside their deadlines. The fourth pushes it to 3300, which is past 3200.", "The longest taken is the 2000-hour filing itself, so it is the one given back and the clock returns to 1300. Three filings lodged.", "Now try a backlog whose first filing is 100 hours due at 100, followed by three 50-hour filings due at 160, 180 and 190. Giving back the first lodges three; skipping the filing in hand lodges two.", "For the settling gap, take three filings across two types with a gap of two: the busiest type appears twice, so the skeleton is one block of three hours plus one final hour, which is four.", "Then take six filings across four types with a gap of one: the skeleton says five, there are six filings and no idle hour, so the answer is six and the clamp is what supplies it."], "tests": "Run the sequencer's suite and read the four parts in order. The second suite cross-checks against an exhaustive search over every subset, and its give-back fixture is what separates the right algorithm from the skipping one. The third suite replays the reported selection through the first part, which is the composition check a shape assertion could not make. The fourth suite compares the arithmetic with an independently written hour-by-hour simulation across several gaps.", "complexity": "The sequencing is O(n log n): one sort, then one heap operation per filing and at most one pop per overrun. The replay is linear. The settling-gap answer is linear in the backlog and independent of the gap, which is the practical reason to prefer the counting bound over a simulation that would tick once per hour and grow with the gap rather than with the work.", "moreTime": "Weighted filings, where a filing carries a penalty for missing rather than counting one each, which breaks the exchange argument and hands the problem to dynamic programming. Then several reviewers, which makes the give-back harder because a filing dropped from one reviewer might fit with another. And release times, where a filing cannot be started before its papers arrive, which is the variant where deadline order stops being sufficient on its own."}, "code": "public struct Filing: Equatable, Sendable {\n    public let id: String\n    public let statutoryType: String\n    public let reviewHours: Int\n    public let deadlineHour: Int\n\n    public init(id: String, statutoryType: String, reviewHours: Int, deadlineHour: Int) {\n        self.id = id\n        self.statutoryType = statutoryType\n        self.reviewHours = reviewHours\n        self.deadlineHour = deadlineHour\n    }\n}\n\npublic struct ScheduleTrace: Equatable, Sendable {\n    public let lodged: [String]\n    public let missed: [String]\n    public let finishHour: Int\n\n    public init(lodged: [String], missed: [String], finishHour: Int) {\n        self.lodged = lodged\n        self.missed = missed\n        self.finishHour = finishHour\n    }\n}\n\npublic enum SequencingError: Error, Equatable, Sendable {\n    case nonPositiveReviewHours(id: String)\n    case nonPositiveDeadline(id: String)\n    case hoursOutOfRange(id: String)\n    case duplicateFilingID(String)\n    case tooManyFilings(Int)\n    case negativeSettlingGap(Int)\n    case settlingGapOutOfRange(Int)\n    case notImplemented\n}\n\n/// A binary max-heap, provided because the standard library has none and\n/// building one is not what this problem is about. Use it, or do not; the\n/// public interface is what the tests read.\npublic struct MaxHeap<Element: Comparable>: Sendable where Element: Sendable {\n    private var storage: [Element] = []\n\n    public init() {}\n\n    public var count: Int { storage.count }\n    public var isEmpty: Bool { storage.isEmpty }\n\n    /// The largest element, without removing it.\n    public var maximum: Element? { storage.first }\n\n    public mutating func insert(_ element: Element) {\n        storage.append(element)\n        var child = storage.count - 1\n        while child > 0 {\n            let parent = (child - 1) / 2\n            guard storage[parent] < storage[child] else { break }\n            storage.swapAt(child, parent)\n            child = parent\n        }\n    }\n\n    public mutating func popMax() -> Element? {\n        guard let largest = storage.first else { return nil }\n        storage.swapAt(0, storage.count - 1)\n        storage.removeLast()\n\n        var parent = 0\n        while true {\n            let left = parent * 2 + 1\n            let right = left + 1\n            var swap = parent\n            if left < storage.count, storage[swap] < storage[left] { swap = left }\n            if right < storage.count, storage[swap] < storage[right] { swap = right }\n            guard swap != parent else { break }\n            storage.swapAt(parent, swap)\n            parent = swap\n        }\n        return largest\n    }\n}\n\npublic struct FilingSequencer: Sendable {\n    /// The backlog is bounded, and so is any single hour figure, so that the\n    /// running clock and the settling-gap arithmetic cannot overflow.\n    public static let maximumFilingCount = 100_000\n    public static let maximumHour = 1_000_000_000\n    public static let maximumSettlingGap = 1_000_000\n\n    public init() {}\n\n    // MARK: Part 1 - Replay a proposed order\n\n    /// What a proposed working order actually achieves.\n    ///\n    /// The trace is deliberately richer than a verdict. Part 2 needs the\n    /// finishing hour to notice an overrun, Part 3's answer is checked by\n    /// replaying it, and a compliance lead needs the missed list to escalate.\n    /// A method returning only whether the order works would force all three\n    /// to simulate the backlog again.\n    public func replay(_ order: [Filing]) throws(SequencingError) -> ScheduleTrace {\n        try validate(order)\n\n        var lodged: [String] = []\n        var missed: [String] = []\n        var clock = 0\n\n        for filing in order {\n            // The work happens either way. A filing lodged late was still\n            // reviewed, and pretending otherwise makes every later hour wrong.\n            clock += filing.reviewHours\n            if clock <= filing.deadlineHour {\n                lodged.append(filing.id)\n            } else {\n                missed.append(filing.id)\n            }\n        }\n\n        return ScheduleTrace(lodged: lodged, missed: missed, finishHour: clock)\n    }\n\n    /// The backlog in statutory deadline order, earliest first, ties by id.\n    ///\n    /// This is the only sort key in the file. Shortest-first is the reflex and\n    /// it is the wrong key: a short filing with a distant deadline can always\n    /// be deferred, while a long filing with a near one cannot.\n    public func orderedByDeadline(_ filings: [Filing]) throws(SequencingError) -> [Filing] {\n        try validate(filings)\n        return filings.sorted { left, right in\n            left.deadlineHour == right.deadlineHour\n                ? left.id < right.id\n                : left.deadlineHour < right.deadlineHour\n        }\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 2 - Maximum filings lodged\n\n    /// The largest number of filings that can be lodged on time.\n    ///\n    /// A projection of the same walk Part 3 reads, so there is one sequencing\n    /// routine in the file rather than two that can disagree.\n    public func maximumLodged(_ filings: [Filing]) throws(SequencingError) -> Int {\n        try sequence(filings).count\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 3 - Report the selection\n\n    /// The filings that get lodged, in the order the reviewer works them.\n    public func selectedFilings(_ filings: [Filing]) throws(SequencingError) -> [String] {\n        try sequence(filings).map(\\.id)\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 4 - Least elapsed time under a settling gap\n\n    /// The fewest elapsed hours, idle included, in which the whole backlog can\n    /// be lodged when two filings of one statutory type must be `settlingGap`\n    /// hours apart.\n    ///\n    /// A different kind of greedy, and worth naming as such: nothing is\n    /// revoked and there is no exchange to argue. The busiest type sets a\n    /// skeleton of `maxCount - 1` blocks of `gap + 1` hours, plus one hour for\n    /// each type tied at that count. Every other filing fits into the idle\n    /// hours the skeleton already contains.\n    ///\n    /// The skeleton is not the whole answer. With enough distinct types there\n    /// is no idle time at all and the schedule is simply as long as the\n    /// backlog, which the skeleton underestimates. The answer is the larger of\n    /// the two, and that clamp is the whole trap.\n    public func leastElapsedHours(\n        _ filings: [Filing],\n        settlingGap: Int\n    ) throws(SequencingError) -> Int {\n        try validate(filings)\n        guard settlingGap >= 0 else { throw .negativeSettlingGap(settlingGap) }\n        guard settlingGap <= Self.maximumSettlingGap else {\n            throw .settlingGapOutOfRange(settlingGap)\n        }\n\n        var perType: [String: Int] = [:]\n        for filing in filings {\n            perType[filing.statutoryType, default: 0] += 1\n        }\n        guard let busiest = perType.values.max() else { return 0 }\n        let tiedAtBusiest = perType.values.filter { $0 == busiest }.count\n\n        let skeleton = (busiest - 1) * (settlingGap + 1) + tiedAtBusiest\n        return max(filings.count, skeleton)\n    }\n\n    // MARK: The one sequencing routine\n\n    /// Deadline order, take everything, and drop the longest filing taken so\n    /// far whenever the clock overruns the deadline in hand.\n    ///\n    /// The exchange argument, which is the reason this is correct: dropping\n    /// the longest keeps the count the same, because one filing came in as one\n    /// went out, and leaves the clock no higher than it stood before the new\n    /// filing arrived. No later decision is ever made worse, so a greedy that\n    /// revokes an earlier choice is still safe. Skipping the current filing\n    /// instead is a different and worse algorithm: it leaves a longer filing\n    /// in place and a higher clock, for the same count.\n    private func sequence(_ filings: [Filing]) throws(SequencingError) -> [Filing] {\n        let ordered = try orderedByDeadline(filings)\n\n        var taken = MaxHeap<TakenFiling>()\n        var clock = 0\n\n        for filing in ordered {\n            clock += filing.reviewHours\n            taken.insert(TakenFiling(reviewHours: filing.reviewHours, id: filing.id))\n\n            if clock > filing.deadlineHour, let longest = taken.popMax() {\n                clock -= longest.reviewHours\n            }\n        }\n\n        var keptIDs = Set<String>()\n        keptIDs.reserveCapacity(taken.count)\n        while let survivor = taken.popMax() {\n            keptIDs.insert(survivor.id)\n        }\n\n        // Deadline order restricted to the survivors, because that is the\n        // order the reviewer actually works them in.\n        return ordered.filter { keptIDs.contains($0.id) }\n    }\n\n    /// A filing currently accepted, ordered by review hours so the heap always\n    /// offers the one worth giving back.\n    private struct TakenFiling: Comparable, Sendable {\n        let reviewHours: Int\n        let id: String\n\n        static func < (lhs: TakenFiling, rhs: TakenFiling) -> Bool {\n            lhs.reviewHours == rhs.reviewHours ? lhs.id < rhs.id : lhs.reviewHours < rhs.reviewHours\n        }\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Shared validation\n\n    /// The precondition every part rests on. The hour ceiling is not\n    /// decoration: an unguarded running clock over a full backlog is the one\n    /// place this file could overflow, and Swift traps on that rather than\n    /// returning a wrong number.\n    private func validate(_ filings: [Filing]) throws(SequencingError) {\n        guard filings.count <= Self.maximumFilingCount else {\n            throw .tooManyFilings(filings.count)\n        }\n        var seen = Set<String>()\n        seen.reserveCapacity(filings.count)\n        for filing in filings {\n            guard filing.reviewHours > 0 else {\n                throw .nonPositiveReviewHours(id: filing.id)\n            }\n            guard filing.deadlineHour > 0 else {\n                throw .nonPositiveDeadline(id: filing.id)\n            }\n            guard filing.reviewHours <= Self.maximumHour,\n                  filing.deadlineHour <= Self.maximumHour else {\n                throw .hoursOutOfRange(id: filing.id)\n            }\n            guard seen.insert(filing.id).inserted else {\n                throw .duplicateFilingID(filing.id)\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n"};
